CESifo Research Network,

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.920
the NBR Development and
Political Economy Group,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.670
the African School of Economics

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:38.907
and the International Crisis Group.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.310
Professor Sviatschi research interests

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:44.670
are labor and development economics,

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.250
with a focus on human capital,
gender balance and crime.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.250
And today she's gonna present her paper

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.758
on market structure and extortion.

00:00:59.758 --> 00:01:04.210
So professor Sviatschi, (indistinct).

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:07.090
- Thank you so much, Teresa
for the presentation.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:09.700
So I'm very happy to
present this new work,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.390
which is done with Zach Brown
and Carlos Schmidt Padilla.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.200
So as many of you know,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.720
organized crime extortion

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.240
are a common aspect of
life in many countries.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.870
This is from developing
to developed countries.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:25.580
So extortion is actually
the main source of revenue

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.837
of criminal organizations in
the particular case of gangs

00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:29.650
that I'm going to talk
today in El Salvador

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.820
is the main source of revenue.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:32.770
And it's estimated

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.620
that about 70% (indistinct) pay extortion.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.070
So this is a big share,

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:40.000
having big implications for the economy.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.930
However, we know very little

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.830
about the effects of gang competition.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.180
So whether gangs are
competing or not on extortion

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.963
and whether they pass
through to consumers.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:50.860
And it's important

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.560
because there are many
instances where a government

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.450
facilitates this corporation
between criminal organizations,

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.080
mainly to reduce violence.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.770
So they induce them to sign these facts

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:04.780
and know some enforcement itself

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.019
may generate instances for gangs

00:02:06.019 --> 00:02:08.033
to collude and not to compete.

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.290
So while we know very
little about this, well,

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.890
the main challenge is
how we measure extortion.

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:17.020
Extortion is very difficult
to measure systematically.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.890
We barely observe it

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.740
and it's very difficult to observe for us

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.080
and also is barely reported

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:24.913
to the police.

00:02:24.913 --> 00:02:25.746
For example,

00:02:25.746 --> 00:02:29.170
in El Salvador, only a very
small share of extortion victims

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.480
ever report incidents to the police.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.120
This is mainly because they don't trust.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.850
They have fear of retaliation.

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.950
So it's very difficult to measure this.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.860
So this means that we don't
have a complete understanding

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.970
about how the economics of extortion works

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.130
for criminal organization.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.140
So today I'm going to try
to answer three questions.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.920
One is how do gangs
determine extortion rates?

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:51.810
So I'm going to present you

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.700
