00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:06.330
And also they have
longstanding delivery contracts

00:42:06.330 --> 00:42:09.685
with retailers that sometimes
when they have renew it,

00:42:09.685 --> 00:42:12.930
they try to negotiate some things there.

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:13.763
But in general,

00:42:13.763 --> 00:42:16.783
they say that that's very
difficult for them to do.

00:42:18.570 --> 00:42:19.654
Okay.

00:42:19.654 --> 00:42:21.550
There are also other things
that can be going on.

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:24.400
One that's very direct is that this part

00:42:24.400 --> 00:42:28.950
also reduce the violence,
increase security,

00:42:28.950 --> 00:42:31.600
because now you don't have
people shooting or homicides.

00:42:31.600 --> 00:42:36.060
So this can have a direct
effect on extortion as well.

00:42:36.060 --> 00:42:39.900
Now they can pay you more because
I'm providing more safety,

00:42:39.900 --> 00:42:41.930
that is also an increase in demand.

00:42:41.930 --> 00:42:44.250
And actually we see whether
with household service,

00:42:44.250 --> 00:42:47.980
whether household income,
expenditures, and also again,

00:42:47.980 --> 00:42:50.180
we take mileage and we
don't see any effect,

00:42:50.180 --> 00:42:51.560
at least in the short run.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:54.700
Remember that all this
is actually just months

00:42:54.700 --> 00:42:57.003
after the pact, we don't find anything.

00:42:57.003 --> 00:42:58.830
It's possible that in the long run,

00:42:58.830 --> 00:43:01.017
there would be a change in demand,

00:43:01.017 --> 00:43:03.467
but at least in the short
run, we are not seeing.

00:43:05.470 --> 00:43:06.560
The other is the one

00:43:06.560 --> 00:43:08.530
that we were just discussing about this,

00:43:08.530 --> 00:43:11.950
whether the firm now is
having less choice now,

00:43:11.950 --> 00:43:14.690
because now it has only
one gang in that territory.

00:43:14.690 --> 00:43:18.920
So they cannot choose after the pact.

00:43:18.920 --> 00:43:20.980
But actually, as I was saying before,

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:22.580
the conversation with the firm highlight

00:43:22.580 --> 00:43:24.900
that firms cannot choose
which gang to pay.

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:27.232
Instead, firms must pay
which gang is in control

00:43:27.232 --> 00:43:28.600
of the tariff.

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:30.320
The best we could do here,

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:32.080
because something that is,

00:43:32.080 --> 00:43:34.690
the bad side of our data
is that we don't have

00:43:34.690 --> 00:43:37.670
for all the period to which gang they pay,

00:43:37.670 --> 00:43:39.372
but we can see it for 2019 data.

00:43:39.372 --> 00:43:42.620
And there we see that they are only paying

00:43:42.620 --> 00:43:43.920
only one gang at the time.

00:43:45.840 --> 00:43:46.760
We also explore,

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:48.810
which are the places that we see

00:43:48.810 --> 00:43:51.260
the highest increase in extortion.

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:54.250
And actually we find
some evidence that gangs

00:43:54.250 --> 00:43:56.140
tend to increase extortion the most

00:43:56.140 --> 00:43:59.250
in this neighborhood with
high downstream demand.

00:43:59.250 --> 00:44:04.120
Again, this is measured
with population density

00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:06.830
and nightlight density as
a measure of development.

00:44:06.830 --> 00:44:08.930
And also we use the total
sales and we see that

00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:09.820
those are the places

00:44:09.820 --> 00:44:12.923
where they are more likely
to be increasing extortion.

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:16.790
So what I have shown you so far,

00:44:16.790 --> 00:44:19.720
I show you that gang colludes,

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:21.750
this increase extortion rates.

00:44:21.750 --> 00:44:24.513
It's higher in places with
higher downstream demand.

00:44:25.380 --> 00:44:27.119
I show some suggestive areas

00:44:27.119 --> 00:44:28.210
that can can be drained

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:32.160
by shifting resources towards extortion,

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:33.900
we also check whether the effects

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:36.363
are robust to alternative cutoffs.

00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:38.880
And we see that more or less,

00:44:38.880 --> 00:44:42.303
it's always a 20% increase in extortion.

00:44:43.650 --> 00:44:45.598
We also try to check

00:44:45.598 --> 00:44:48.110
whether the effects replicate,

00:44:48.110 --> 00:44:49.850
because right now we are doing organized

00:44:49.850 --> 00:44:50.857
and municipality route level.

00:44:50.857 --> 00:44:53.230
And we try to check
what happens if we look

00:44:53.230 --> 00:44:55.350
at the neighborhood level
at the Canton level,

00:44:55.350 --> 00:44:57.810
which would be a bit more similar

00:44:57.810 --> 00:44:59.680
to neighborhood level, municipality,

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:03.460
you can think as county like
in the US and Canton level

00:45:03.460 --> 00:45:05.360
could be more like neighborhood.

00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:08.233
And we see the effects
replicate pretty well there.

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:12.440
So now let's see what happens,

00:45:12.440 --> 00:45:14.770
which is the firm response
to this extortion?

00:45:14.770 --> 00:45:17.650
So what are the downstream
consequences of extortion?

00:45:17.650 --> 00:45:20.040
And here we have two approaches,

00:45:20.040 --> 00:45:24.020
which are mainly limited
by the data that we have.

00:45:24.020 --> 00:45:27.613
The first one is we could
use distributor's sales data,

00:45:28.674 --> 00:45:32.730
which has this distribution
margin, which is basically

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:34.850
the difference between
the revenue they pay

00:45:34.850 --> 00:45:35.880
and the procurement cost,

00:45:35.880 --> 00:45:37.096
how much they're paying the multinational.

00:45:37.096 --> 00:45:40.650
And we can think about
this as the delivery fee,

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:42.063
what they pay for delivery.

00:45:43.140 --> 00:45:44.580
With pharmacy sales data,

00:45:44.580 --> 00:45:46.970
what I was going to show
you is that we can do better

00:45:49.496 --> 00:45:50.329
because with pharmacy sales data,

00:45:50.329 --> 00:45:52.081
we cut access to other admin data sets

00:45:52.081 --> 00:45:53.490
that has the prices for product.

00:45:53.490 --> 00:45:56.219
So there we can see
whether prices respond,

00:45:56.219 --> 00:45:57.270
but let's see first

00:45:58.770 --> 00:46:01.370
what they are doing to these
retailers (indistinct).

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:04.130
So the first thing that we do is again,

00:46:04.130 --> 00:46:07.120
the reduced form where now
the outcome of interest

00:46:07.120 --> 00:46:07.986
is the log decision margin.

00:46:07.986 --> 00:46:10.077
Just think about this as (indistinct),

00:46:12.213 --> 00:46:13.487
and what we see is that,

00:46:13.487 --> 00:46:15.900
when banks cooperate, after the pact,

00:46:15.900 --> 00:46:19.210
there is an increase of
12% in this recent margins,

00:46:19.210 --> 00:46:21.610
especially for those deliveries that occur

00:46:21.610 --> 00:46:23.290
closest to the extortion payments.

00:46:23.290 --> 00:46:26.040
So the ones that are
linked to that extortion.

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:29.730
We also do the IV, but this is only again,

00:46:29.730 --> 00:46:31.657
since we know that this could not be

00:46:31.657 --> 00:46:35.237
the only mechanism, is just
to quantify the effects.

00:46:35.237 --> 00:46:36.070
And here,

00:46:36.070 --> 00:46:40.070
what we see is that for
$1 increase in extortion,

00:46:40.070 --> 00:46:43.970
this increases the firm
distribution margin by 0.8

00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:46.670
for the deliveries that are
closest to extortion pact.

00:46:47.700 --> 00:46:49.257
So this is very high.

00:46:50.503 --> 00:46:52.107
Then we also wanted to see, okay,

00:46:52.107 --> 00:46:55.070
are there heterogenous effects in increase

00:46:55.070 --> 00:46:56.713
in extortion by product type?

00:46:58.450 --> 00:47:02.560
And remember as similar as
you saw in the descriptives,

00:47:02.560 --> 00:47:04.170
it's not that increase in extortion

00:47:04.170 --> 00:47:07.120
varies by the type of products.

00:47:07.120 --> 00:47:09.790
However, when we check
the distribution margin,

00:47:09.790 --> 00:47:11.090
how much they pay, (indistinct),

00:47:11.090 --> 00:47:13.953
there is some evidence at this suggestive

00:47:13.953 --> 00:47:18.090
that there is a larger increase
for more elastic products.

00:47:21.910 --> 00:47:25.010
So this is just suggestive,

00:47:25.010 --> 00:47:26.470
but at least something

00:47:26.470 --> 00:47:28.040
that basically a distribution company,

00:47:28.040 --> 00:47:29.521
they have to increase the cost.

00:47:29.521 --> 00:47:33.013
They do it much more for these retailers.

00:47:34.840 --> 00:47:37.740
We also check these other
firm responses to extortion.

00:47:37.740 --> 00:47:38.573
For example,

00:47:38.573 --> 00:47:39.406
the extent margins

00:47:39.406 --> 00:47:41.420
were just before Rosella was asking

00:47:41.420 --> 00:47:45.580
whether they change the number
of deliveries, nothing there,

00:47:45.580 --> 00:47:47.050
the total cost that they pay.

00:47:47.050 --> 00:47:49.420
If this is during now higher demand,

00:47:49.420 --> 00:47:50.345
there should be an
increase also in the cost

00:47:50.345 --> 00:47:53.500
because they are demanding
more from the multinationals,

00:47:53.500 --> 00:47:54.460
nothing there,

00:47:54.460 --> 00:47:57.220
the number of unique
products, unique retailers,

00:47:57.220 --> 00:48:00.530
no evidence of a change of
the distribution company

00:48:00.530 --> 00:48:01.780
and the extensive margin.

00:48:04.190 --> 00:48:07.930
So the next thing that
we wanted to to know is,

00:48:07.930 --> 00:48:10.700
use this pharmacy data, as
I was telling you before,

00:48:10.700 --> 00:48:13.440
another way that we can see the consumers

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:14.620
is by checking prices.

00:48:14.620 --> 00:48:15.492
The problem with data

00:48:15.492 --> 00:48:17.030
that we have with the
distribution company is that

00:48:17.030 --> 00:48:19.200
we don't have the prices

00:48:19.200 --> 00:48:21.770
that all these retailers
are selling to consumers.

00:48:21.770 --> 00:48:24.093
So we use this data on pharmacy sales,

00:48:25.510 --> 00:48:27.380
which is a subset of the market

00:48:27.380 --> 00:48:29.493
that the distribution company delivers.

00:48:30.750 --> 00:48:33.025
And interestingly, in
this case of El Salvador,

00:48:33.025 --> 00:48:36.240
they have the highest drug
prices in Central America.

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:38.480
So this potentially
reduces access to drugs.

00:48:38.480 --> 00:48:40.470
So it's important for us
actually to understand

00:48:40.470 --> 00:48:44.050
why they have this very high prices.

00:48:44.050 --> 00:48:45.380
So what we have,

00:48:45.380 --> 00:48:46.880
and actually this is important,

00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:48.610
because you may wonder why are they,

00:48:48.610 --> 00:48:51.410
why the government is
collecting all this data?

00:48:51.410 --> 00:48:52.760
And actually is because of this

00:48:52.760 --> 00:48:55.680
is that they are having
all this information now,

00:48:55.680 --> 00:48:58.080
which is basically national
directory of medicines

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:01.150
of El Salvador from 2014 to 2017,

00:49:01.150 --> 00:49:04.750
which has super detail data on quantity,

00:49:04.750 --> 00:49:08.180
revenue by pharmacy for
over all the products

00:49:08.180 --> 00:49:11.160
refined as molecular brand size.

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:14.430
This is very rich data we
want to work with pharmacy

00:49:14.430 --> 00:49:16.263
and El Salvador is pretty good.

00:49:17.700 --> 00:49:21.100
So what we do from this data
is we take out the prices.

00:49:21.100 --> 00:49:21.933
So here,

00:49:21.933 --> 00:49:24.220
the first thing that we try to see is,

00:49:24.220 --> 00:49:27.550
do prices increase after
the non aggression pact,

00:49:27.550 --> 00:49:28.873
the prices of these drugs.

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:30.820
And we see that, yes,

00:49:30.820 --> 00:49:35.300
we see about an 8% increase
in prices for pharmaceuticals.

00:49:35.300 --> 00:49:37.300
We try to see whether there
are differential effects,

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:40.670
whether you are distributed
by our company or others,

00:49:40.670 --> 00:49:43.320
just to try to see whether
this is something particular

00:49:43.320 --> 00:49:44.153
for our distribution company or not.

00:49:44.153 --> 00:49:46.350
And we don't see any differential effect,

00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:48.690
which is consistent with what
I was telling you before that,

00:49:48.690 --> 00:49:50.515
when we talk to these other
companies that even though

00:49:50.515 --> 00:49:51.950
they didn't want to share the data,

00:49:51.950 --> 00:49:54.450
they talk to us about how this works,

00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:55.900
and it was very similar.

00:49:55.900 --> 00:49:58.475
The mechanisms that they
have, having the security team

00:49:58.475 --> 00:50:00.580
that would negotiate with gangs.

00:50:00.580 --> 00:50:03.103
So that's why we are not
seeing differential effects.

00:50:04.290 --> 00:50:07.140
We also check, and this,

00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:09.470
the price for drugs for chronic diagnosis,

00:50:09.470 --> 00:50:12.800
because these are the, as the name says,

00:50:12.800 --> 00:50:15.580
they are like illnesses that
you really need the meds.

00:50:15.580 --> 00:50:19.890
It's like the abilities
or respiratory problems.

00:50:19.890 --> 00:50:21.790
And we do this because
we wanted to see, okay,

00:50:21.790 --> 00:50:26.090
how much this increasing prices
end up affecting consumers.

